Routing Conventions:

1. All routes must live inside the app folder
2. Route files must be named either page.js or page.tsx
   3.each folder represents a segment of the URL path

when the conventions are followed, the file automatically becomes availabe as a route

File based routing::

Scenario 1:
we want to create a home page that shows up when someone visits our site at loclahost:3000

Scenario 2:
we need to create Two additional routes

1. for about
2. for profile

## Nested Routes

Scenario 3:
we need to set up that shows up when useres visits loclahost3000/blog we will also need to handle two additional routes
3.1 loclahost:3000/blog/first
3.2 loclahost:3000/blog/secound

## Dynamic Routes:

Scenario 4:
building a product listing and Details page when the user visit loclahost:300/products they should see a list of three products however when they navigate
to loclahost:3000/products/id followed by the products id they should see details for that specific product

example:
navigating to loclahost:3000/products/1 should display details about the first product

## Nested Dynamic Routes:

Scenario 5:
you want to show details at /products/1 but you also need to display the first review for the product at /products/1/reviews/1

## Catch-all Segments:

scenario 6:
imagine we're buillding a documentation site for our project this site has a multiple features and each feature contains several Consepts that need documentation picture a side navigation with the five features where the first features expands to show five different concepts the goal is to create a unique routes for each concept under its respective features think about URLs like loclahost:3000/Feature1/concept1
![alt text](image.png)

## Not-Found-Page:

when you visit a route that doesn't exist in your app folder you will see a basic 404 pages

## File Colocation:

each folder represents a route segment that maps to a URL path but here is the important part a route only becomes publicly accessible when you add a page.js or page.tsx file
to it

## Private folders:

Private folders are super useful for a bunch of things:
1-Keep your UI logc seperate from routing logic
2-Having a consistent way to organize internal files in your project
3-Making it easier to group related files in your code editor
4-Avoiding potential naming conflicts with future Next.js file naming conventions
